    "explanation": "This table compares different computer programs, called parsers, that analyze sentence structure, showing how well they perform. The first column lists the name of each parser, often named after the researchers who developed them or the type of technology used, like \"Transformer.\" The second column, \"Training,\" explains how each parser was taught: \"WSJ only, discriminative\" means it learned from a specific collection of newspaper text (the Wall Street Journal) using a focused learning approach, while \"semi-supervised\" means it learned from that same newspaper text but also used a larger amount of general text without direct answers to improve its understanding. The \"WSJ 23 F1\" column shows a performance score, where higher numbers indicate that the parser was more accurate at correctly identifying the grammatical structure of sentences on a specific test from the Wall Street Journal. Looking at these scores, we can see that parsers generally achieve better accuracy when they are trained using the \"semi-supervised\" method, with the \"Transformer (4 layers)\" parser achieving the highest score of 92.7, suggesting that learning from a broader range of text helps these programs understand language more effectively."
